    {
      "id": "msg_07f42c22-b6d0-4c60-934f-da5abd5c2530",
      "role": "user",
      "created": 1790144818,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "toolResponse",
          "id": "call_00_SAUfyl3JnhnH77jvGx8L2394",
          "toolResult": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "resultType": "complete",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "# Loaded: lore-stack-index/references/agent-systems.md\n\n# agent systems stack\n\nSeparate layers, each owning one kind of authority or context. **The portfolio only becomes legible when agents preserve those boundaries.**\n\n## The core four\n\n* **umbra** - the generic occlusion engine, and the renamed successor to **cli-guard**, which now 301-redirects. Policy lives in a KDL guardfile rather than in code. It validates argv before `execve`, scopes each verb, prunes every upstream surface to what was granted, and audits every call to rotating JSONL. Its `umbra` CLI builds standalone guarded CLIs and native agent skills from that policy, and was called `specgen` until umbra v0.192.0. The dirty-tree gate and the egress proxy left with ward's surface.\n* **mcp-beaver** - renders an umbra guardfile into a guarded MCP server and HTTP tool API inside one generic OCI image. One runtime, many guardfiles. An undeclared operation has no handler at all in spec mode. **Downstream of umbra, never a peer to it.**\n* **housecast** - the roster and composition engine, and the eval runner. One YAML roster declares every role's meld, boundary allocation and invariant, emits an immutable bundle, then grades that exact bundle. It absorbed the engine and `evalkit` from agent-compose on 2026-08-27 and was proved byte-identical to the Go compositor, which is being deleted, so **agent-compose is downstream of housecast rather than the other way round.** MIT, v0.3.0.\n* **agent-compose** - selects and compiles roles, personalities, skills, harness context, model-class pruning, and native MCP projections. Changes what an agent knows and how it is framed. **Grants no executable authority, and role selection never unions tool permissions.**\n\n## Surrounding layers\n\n## Role boundaries persuade, they do not refuse\n\n**MEASURED 2026-09-15** against agent-compose, an advocate bundle against a\nscience bundle, taken at `7200bb1` and re-verified at the tip `a9353a2`.\n\n**Two things bind, and neither is a tool permission.**\n\n**Skill mounting**, which is what a seat can read. 110 advocate skills against\n96 science, 65 shared, and a seat cannot read what was never delivered.\n\n**Boundary composition integrity**, in `internal/resolver/resolver.go` at\n`composedBoundaries`. **Four refusals**, each naming the role. A deployment\ncannot omit a boundary that is undefined, cannot omit one the role **owns**,\ncannot omit one the role **holds within a scope**, and cannot omit one the\nrole **never carried**, which closes the case where a deployment drops\nsomething absent and reads as having narrowed itself. The reason is in the\ncode: a scoped grant is a bounded permission rather than a deferral, so\ndropping it widens the role instead of narrowing the deployment. **This is\nreal structural enforcement**, and it matters past its own scope: the estate\nalready knows how to enforce a boundary in the composer, so per-role gating\nwould not be a greenfield build if it is ever revisited.\n\n**It does not reach scope content.** Every refusal matches on a name or an\nowner, and none reads `scoped.Scope`. A boundary cannot be dropped, cannot be\nre-owned, cannot be omitted where absent, and its scope text can say anything\nat all. So a boundary cannot be\ndropped and its scope can say anything at all. Everything else is identical: the whole\npermissions block, 10 allow and 1 deny each, 27 MCP registries with no\nrole-only entries either way, and a composer grep for `permissions`,\n`allowedTools` or `disallowedTools` returning zero. The per-role Playwright\nprofiles separate browser state rather than restricting who drives which.\nAOSguard is a separate layer and gates uniformly: a grep for role names across\nits 13 guardfiles returns zero, so it constrains what may be done on the\nestate rather than who may do it.\n\n**Nobody constructed a deception, and the line above already said so.** Every\ncharter states that its doctrine grants no commands, credentials, network\naccess, or executable permission. **The drift is in the reading**, and seats\nincluding the director have reasoned as though boundary text were a control\nthat would refuse when it is a control that only persuades.\n\n**Decision, 2026-09-15: do not build per-role action gating.** Filed as\n`teable:coilyco-flight-deck/agent-compose#7742`. The incident that raised the\nquestion was a seat fabricating an attribution in prose to a peer, and **no\npermission system gates what an agent writes to another agent**, so the\nproposed mechanism would not have caught the thing that prompted it. The\nfailure was citation discipline, which took a doctrine fix. **The decision\nrests on that one leg and no other.**\n\n**A second leg was withdrawn the same evening.** This entry briefly argued\nthat the compliance control had held under temptation, on five narrow\nreadings by one seat. That is not a compliance rate: n=5, self-reported by the\nsubject, the subject knew it was under measurement for four of the five, and\nthe ratified text already said it would read narrow. It was never evidence\nabout restraint under an expansive grant, which is what the argument needed.\n**A decision resting partly on a number tagged EXPECTED inherits the tag.**\n\n**Reverses on the first observation of a seat taking a TOOL ACTION outside its\ncharter, and that condition cannot fire today.** MEASURED on this host: one\n`PreToolUse` hook exists, it matches `Bash` alone, its command does git\nattribution rather than charter auditing, and it writes no log. Every other\nhook event has an empty matcher. The two logs under `~/.claude` are `Stop`\nhooks reading the agent's own prose. With permissions and MCP registries\nbyte-identical across roles, **nothing would deny, log, or notice an\nout-of-charter call.** Transcripts hold the record, so finding one requires\nalready suspecting it. Record present, detection absent, and those are\ndifferent claims.\n\n**So an audit is approved, which is not the gate that was declined.** Log tool\ncalls with the role attribution already in the environment, since\n`AGENT_GIT_ATTRIBUTION_ROLE` is set per bundle. It gates nothing and changes no\npermission. Declining a gate is compatible with wanting a smoke detector, and\nuntil it exists the reversal condition above is aspirational rather than\nobservable.\n\n**Not every audit discharges it, and the obvious first increment does not.**\nAn argument-free log of role, tool and timestamp does not make an\nout-of-charter call visible, because **tool names carry the verb on MCP\nsurfaces and not on the core tools**. `kubectl get` and `kubectl apply` both\nlog as `Bash`, and an in-charter `Edit` and an out-of-charter `Edit` are one\nline, which is where most charter-relevant action here actually runs. Shipping\nthat increment is fine. **Describing it as satisfying the reversal condition\nis not**, because the decision then reads reversible while staying\nunobservable, which is the precise defect the director guardrail's instrument\ntest was added to catch. What discharges it is a two-field allowlist, command\nname and target path, which fails closed and is what the charters are defined\nover.\n\n**Size confidence to that.** A persuasive control a compliant agent follows is\na real control. A single confused or non-compliant seat has nothing behind it,\nand nothing watching either.\n\n* **AOS** - public integration and launch surface, composing agent-compose, shared operating rules, catalog validation, the dev-base image, and AOSguard.\n* **AOSguard** - guarded operator CLI for Forgejo, AWS, Kubernetes, Tailscale. **AOSguard owns operator commands. Do not invent a `ward ops` surface.**\n* **agentic-os-kai** - Kai-specific deploy context and composition inputs. Owns no generic runtime behavior.\n* **agentic-os-hardware** measures the fleet and selects model lanes, **Agent Proxy** and deploy-owned serving make them available, and **Sirens Echo** is the clearest applied harness.\n\n## Ward is archived, not deleted\n\nWard built governed execution for unattended coding agents in containers. **It is archived.** The dispatch record ends 2026-06-18, the repo went quiet on 2026-08-17, and its final commit moved Ward's own dev verbs off `ward exec` onto a justfile. Kai settled the posture in two steps: freeze the runtime on 2026-08-24, then confirm archival on 2026-08-28, with key components extracted to a document and configs being deprovisioned across the fleet. **Its path still resolves, which is the failure shape to watch**, since a stale reference returns 200 and hides the drift instead of failing loudly.\n\nWhat survives in daily use is the **workflow lane vocabulary** in `ward:` AGENTS.md frontmatter, read by composition rather than by any Ward process: `merge-remote-main`, `pull-request`, `pull-request-and-merge`, `remote-branch-only`. The `ward:` key stays because renaming it fleet-wide is cost without gain.\n\n**Never describe Ward as an active layer or propose new Ward features.** It carries no tracker rows and no entry in the repository index. Anything specified as a Ward run is dead rather than paused, including the recorded issue-to-production demo: a headless build can still produce an autonomous-delivery artifact, but it cannot be that one.\n\n## The dichotomy underneath\n\nEvery autonomous-engineering system needs **productive motion** and **bounded authority**. Build one half and you discover the other through either stalled work or unsafe work.\n\n* **Developer half** - inner-loop velocity. agent-compose supplies the framing, and native session shadows and linked worktrees supply the isolation Ward's containers used to.\n* **Operator half** - outer-loop safety, the thing that says no. umbra supplies the engine, mcp-beaver serves it to agents, AOSguard covers operator commands.\n\nVelocity without a wall is reckless. A wall without velocity is bureaucracy. **The estate runs both halves as one system, and that is the point.** Ward's half was absorbed instead of abandoned, so the claim holds and its evidence moved.\n\n**Positioning line:** \"I know where these layers break against each other because I've spent real time running them as one system, on hardware I own.\" Pattern recognition earned by operating the whole stack, not by credentials.\n\n**What the line may not claim.** No novelty, no scarcity, no rare form. A category count on 2026-09-10 found both halves commodity. The MCP filtering-proxy half returns 1,950 repositories on the gateway, proxy, policy and allowlist query, with `IBM/mcp-context-forge` at 4,455 stars and `Nasiko-Labs/nasiko` at 6,621. The argv-guard half carries `Dicklesworthstone/destructive_command_guard` at 5,952, `sheeki03/tirith` at 2,707 and `anthropics/sandbox-runtime` at 5,198. Every one of those is a component, and what the counts leave standing is integration, so the line claims four layers running together on real hardware and stops there. Reasoning and reversal condition in `teable:coilyco-flight-deck/agentic-os#7401`.\n\nRelated: `lore-method-harness-engineering`, `lore-project-portfolio`.\n\n\n---\nFile loaded into context."
                }
