        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "Now I have a clear picture:\n\n- **acompose** = agent-compose. Selects and compiles roles, personalities, skills, harness context, model-class pruning, and native MCP projections. Changes what an agent knows and how it's framed. Grants no executable authority. This is the \"framing\" layer — like a compiler for agent bundles.\n- **umbra** = the generic occlusion engine. Policy in KDL guardfile. Validates argv before execve, scopes verbs, prunes upstream surfaces, audits to rotating JSONL. The `umbra` CLI builds standalone guarded CLIs and native agent skills from policy.\n\nBoth are in the \"agent systems core\" lane. Neither is a flagship. mcp-beaver is explicitly closed as a product line (2026-09-02), and the generation half is commoditized.\n\nThe key facts for this pricing question:\n1. Both are OSS. housecast is MIT v0.3.0. umbra is the renamed successor to cli-guard.\n2. No flagship, no customers, no revenue. The portfolio explicitly says \"Do not claim a flagship, business, customer base, or single product unless newer evidence establishes one.\"\n3. The agent-systems.md explicitly notes on 2026-09-10: category count found BOTH halves commodity. The MCP filtering-proxy half returns 1,950 repos. The argv-guard half: destructive_command_guard at 5,952 stars, tirith at 2,707, sandbox-runtime at 5,198. So umbra (argv-guard / occlusion engine) is a commodity category with established OSS competitors. agent-compose's composition/framing half is also commodity.\n4. mcp-beaver (downstream of umbra) is infrastructure not a product, and the product line is closed.\n\nSo the honest answer is:\n- Neither has a buyer base. Both are commodity categories with free OSS alternatives that have thousands of stars.\n- The \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" framing is the wrong model for these, because:\n  - umbra's value is integration (four layers running together on real hardware), which is a consulting/service framing, but the category is commodity and there are free, more-starred alternatives.\n  - agent-compose's value is framing/roster compilation, which is also commodity, and it grants no executable authority.\n\nNow, my guardrail says: strongest case against FIRST, then reversal condition, then verdict.\n\nThe user asked: \"What might someone pay for a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of acompose or umbra? Give me price bands for each, and say whether anyone would actually buy one.\"\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Give price bands for each.\n2. Say whether anyone would actually buy one.\n\nThe outward personality says I should WebSearch to check my claims about pricing/market before asserting \"good, slow, expensive, novel, or popular.\" The role run says \"WebSearch the claim before ranking it, and report how many independent results came back.\" And \"WebSearch the comparison before any claim that something is good, slow, expensive, novel, or popular.\"\n\nI'm making market/pricing claims. I should WebSearch to check:\n- What people pay for custom agent-security / command-guard tooling or consulting.\n- What people pay for agent-composition / harness tooling.\n\nActually, the more relevant search is: is there a market for \"argv guard\" / \"command guard\" / \"MCP gateway\" consulting, and what do comparable OSS tools charge for support/enterprise.\n\nBut let me be careful about what I'm actually claiming. The core claims:\n1. Both are commodity categories with free OSS alternatives (this is already in the lore, sourced with category counts from 2026-09-10). That's locally sourced and I can cite it.\n2. Whether anyone would buy: no one has expressed interest; no customers exist; portfolio explicitly forbids claiming customers/revenue.\n\nLet me do a quick WebSearch to check the outside frame for pricing. The boundary-seek-external-validation says I own reaching outside. Let me actually do a search to ground the price bands rather than invent them.\n\nActually, let me think about what searches would actually be productive:\n- \"command guard\" / \"argv guard\" — these are commodity. Pricing for these as a custom cut + support: likely low, because free alternatives exist.\n- agent-compose: composition/framing — commodity too.\n\nThe honest answer, applying the guardrail and outward check: nobody would buy a \"custom one-time cut + long-term support\" of either, because both are commodity categories where the free OSS reference implementations have thousands of stars and the buyer would just use the free one. The price band is therefore essentially \"$0 to low four figures\" as a services engagement, not a product, and the real value (integration across the four layers) isn't what \"custom cut + support\" of a single layer captures.\n\nBut I should ground this with an actual outside search, per my role. Let me do a couple of searches.\n\nLet me search for:\n1. What free/OSS alternatives and their traction (to confirm commodity). Actually the lore already has this. But the role says I should report independent result counts from my own WebSearch, not just cite the lore's 2026-09-10 numbers.\n2. Pricing for comparable consulting / enterprise support.\n\nLet me do the WebSearch tool. Wait — do I have a web-search skill/tool? Looking at my tools: I have \"web-search\" skill available, and there's a `shell` tool. Actually the skill list includes \"web-search - Search the web and extract page content using DuckDuckGo\". But is there an actual function/tool? Looking at my available functions, I see `shell`, `edit`, `write`, `load`, `load_skill`, `read_image`, `tree`, `analyze`, `todo__todo_write`, delegate, apps tools, extensionmanager tools, kai-server MCP tools.\n\nThere's no direct WebSearch function in my tool list. The \"web-search\" is a skill. Let me load it to see if it provides a way to search. Actually, the skill \"web-search\" would give me instructions on how to search, possibly via shell with curl to DuckDuckGo.\n\nLet me check: the boundary says I own reaching outside. But I don't have a direct web tool exposed as a function. The web-search skill may instruct me to use curl or a specific approach. Let me load it.\n\nActually, let me think about time budget. I have plenty. Let me load the web-search skill and then do actual searches to ground the pricing bands. This is what the role demands — \"going to get it is your work.\"\n\nLet me load web-search skill.",
