          "text": "**Call first: no one would buy a custom one-time cut plus long-term support of either acompose or umbra as they stand today.** The one fact that decides it: both sit in commodity categories with free, more-starred open-source alternatives and funded vendors, and the portfolio has zero adoption and zero inbound demand. Reversal: an actual inbound request naming a budget, or real adoption evidence.\n\n## The strongest case against my call\n\nThis is the part that could be wrong, and it's sourced outside the proposal. The security/compliance category demonstrably has paying enterprise buyers: Chainguard, GuardCMD, CommandGuard, ToolGuard, and Securiti all charge real money for command/argv policy and MCP-gateway guarding. So umbra — KDL guardfile, argv-validated before `execve`, rotating JSONL audit — is shaped like a thing enterprises already pay for. \"The category pays\" is true, and it's the strongest reason to think a buyer exists.\n\n**Why it still loses:** the buyers in that category buy from funded vendors with SOC 2, support SLAs, and a company behind them. \"The category is monetized\" does not imply \"a single-maintainer OSS project with no adoptable artifact has a buyer.\" That inference is the seam — mark it: the category moves money, the project does not, and the two are different claims.\n\n## Price bands (what a comparable services engagement costs, not what anyone has offered)\n\nNo customer exists, so these are category-anchored figures, not demand. I'm not manufacturing revenue — nothing below implies anyone has paid or asked.\n\n| | One-time custom cut | Long-term support |\n|---|---|---|\n| **umbra** (occlusion engine / argv guard) | $10k–$40k | $1.5k–$4k/mo |\n| **acompose** (agent-compose, framing/composition) | $5k–$20k | $1k–$3k/mo |\n\numbra bands higher because security tooling is the one of the two categories where enterprise money actually changes hands. acompose bands lower because framing/orchestration is the most commoditized of all — the buyer's free alternatives (LangChain, CrewAI, AutoGen, AWS Bedrock) are the strongest and most maintained.\n\n## Would anyone actually buy one\n\nNo, in the current state, for three reasons:\n\n1. **Both halves are commodity, and that's measured, not a vibe.** The lore's 2026-09-10 category count found the argv-guard half (`destructive_command_guard` at 5,952 stars, `tirith` at 2,707, `sandbox-runtime` at 5,198) and the MCP-filtering half (~1,950 repos) already crowded. My own search this session, four queries and roughly two dozen results, turns up funded vendors and open-source gateways everywhere — Microsoft `mcp-gateway`, IBM ContextForge, Obot, ToolGuard, Blackfrost `agentguard-mcp`. There is no scarcity to charge against.\n\n2. **There's no installed base to support.** \"Long-term support\" presupposes someone adopted the thing. Neither project has customers, stars-driven adoption, or a named org using it. The portfolio record is explicit: no flagship, no customer base, and the adoptability blocker is the same shape as Agent Proxy's — the artifact has to actually be adoptable (LICENSE, homepage) before anyone can even choose it.\n\n3. **A custom cut of one layer misses the only defensible value.** The positioning that survives the commodity finding is integration — four layers running together on hardware Kai owns. That's consulting, not \"cut + support of umbra alone.\" A buyer who wanted that would be buying the integration story, and even that has no expressed demand.\n\n**What would reverse it** (and it's observable, not aspirational): a real inbound request naming a budget; or adoption evidence — an org using either in production; or the artifacts becoming adoptable (LICENSE + homepage published). None of those has fired, and until one does, \"custom cut + support\" is a lead-gen story with no lead.\n\nWhat I'm not doing: I'm not recommending you build a support business around either, and I'm not claiming the code is why — it's the category, which is a position, not a defect."
